! [33mClickHouseSink/default records the address clickhouse.kuberecord-quickstart.svc:9000.[0m

[33mThat name resolves inside the cluster and nowhere else, so discovery was right and so is[0m
[33mthe sink: this machine is simply outside it. kuberecord reads a cluster and never acts on[0m
[33mone, so it will not forward a port for you.[0m

[33mForward it yourself, then re-run against the forwarded address:[0m

[1m    kubectl port-forward -n kuberecord-quickstart svc/clickhouse 9000:9000[0m
[1m    kuberecord timeline … --sink-addr 127.0.0.1:9000[0m

[33mOr write it down once, and every later invocation reads it:[0m

[1m    kuberecord config set-profile local --from-sink ClickHouseSink/default[0m
[1m    kuberecord config use-profile local[0m

[33mThat reads this same sink, records 127.0.0.1:9000 in place of the address above,[0m
[33mand takes the database and the user from it. The forward is still yours to run:[0m
[33ma profile records an address, it does not open a tunnel.[0m

[33mExport KUBERECORD_CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD first: it is the variable that profile[0m
[33mrecords, and the password it wants is the sink's own user's — a credential that can[0m
[33mwrite to the audit trail. Add --username <read-only user> to the set-profile line[0m
[33mabove to read as one instead, and it records a variable of its own.[0m

[33mBoth routes, and why this tool will not forward the port for you:[0m
[33mdocs/CLI.md#running-the-cli-outside-the-cluster[0m
